    {
      "name": "the released voice repeats a number it spent, and is answered",
      "law": "Keys, records and standings",
      "obligations": ["VII-8", "XIV-10"],
      "derivation": "The same voice asks again **on the number it already spent two exchanges ago**, when it was still a holder and the mark moved to 2. It holds no standing anywhere now, so Article XII step 7 gives it the stranger's case — \"no standing anywhere ... the describe of whatever the warden's own public being exposes\" — and Article XIV says what that costs it: \"A stranger spends nothing. It has no row, so no mark is kept for it and its numbers are not counted.\" **So a replayed number is answered here, and that is the only thing in the suite that can tell release from a row kept empty.** The estate cannot: a row holding no beings reaches nothing but the public being, which is byte-for-byte what a stranger is given. The record cannot either: a cargo is read for one being, and a row that no longer names that being is absent from it whether it was released or merely emptied. **What separates them is the window.** A door that kept the empty row still keeps that voice's mark at 2 and must refuse this number as a replay; a door that released it has nothing to judge the number against and answers. One is bytes and the other is silence.",
